This shallowEqual function is meant to back a custom React.memo comparator, but it has a bug: it returns true for two objects that have different numbers of keys, as long as the keys they DO share match. Fix it and implement solve(input) in JavaScript. input is a JSON string array of exactly two objects. Return "true" or "false" (as a JSON string) indicating whether the objects are shallowly equal (same key count AND same key/value pairs at the top level).

Buggy version:
function shallowEqual(a, b) {
  for (const key in a) {
    if (a[key] !== b[key]) return false;
  }
  return true;
}

This class uses a mutable default argument, so every Cart instance that doesn't pass items ends up sharing the same underlying list, causing cross-instance state bugs. Fix it, then implement solve(input) in Python. input is a JSON string: {"a": [items to add to cart A], "b": [items to add to cart B]}. Create two separate Cart instances (no items passed to the constructor), add the given items to each, and return a JSON string of {"a": cart_a.items, "b": cart_b.items} proving the two carts are isolated.

Buggy version:
class Cart:
    def __init__(self, items=[]):
        self.items = items
    def add(self, item):
        self.items.append(item)
